• if notes[i] = notes[i+1] then b_i ≠ b_{i+1}.

The key is to notice that the only forbidden transitions are:
• b_i = b_{i+1} when notes[i] = notes[i+1],
• b_i < b_{i+1} when notes[i] > notes[i+1],
• b_i > b_{i+1} when notes[i] < notes[i+1].

We can use a simple greedy approach:
1. Start with b_1 = 1.
2. For each i from 1 to n-1:
   - If notes[i] < notes[i+1], set b_{i+1} = b_i + 1 (mod 5).
   - If notes[i] > notes[i+1], set b_{i+1} = b_i - 1 (mod 5).
   - If notes[i] = notes[i+1], set b_{i+1} = b_i + 1 (mod 5) if b_i = 1, else b_{i+1} = b_i - 1 (mod 5).

This ensures no two consecutive fingers are the same when notes are equal, and the direction of change matches the direction of the notes.

If at any point we get b_{i+1} = b_i, we return -1.

